Ran into similar issues, it seems that the MSAA variant (`get_texture('render_buffers', 'color_msaa')`) does not have a storage bit set.
A peek into `servers/rendering/renderer_rd/storage_rd/render_scene_buffers_rd.cpp` shows that this is indeed the case:
```cpp
uint32_t RenderSceneBuffersRD::get_color_usage_bits(bool p_resolve, bool p_msaa, bool p_storage) {
	DEV_ASSERT((!p_resolve && !p_msaa) || (p_resolve != p_msaa));

	uint32_t usage_bits = RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_SAMPLING_BIT | RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_COLOR_ATTACHMENT_BIT | RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_INPUT_ATTACHMENT_BIT;
	if (p_msaa) {
		usage_bits |= RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_CAN_COPY_FROM_BIT;
	} else if (p_resolve) {
		usage_bits |= RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_CAN_COPY_TO_BIT | (p_storage ? RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_STORAGE_BIT : 0);
	} else {
		usage_bits |= (p_storage ? RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_STORAGE_BIT : 0);
	}

	return usage_bits;
}
```
Similar things are happening for depth buffer. It seems that you only have the storage bit set for the buffer containing resolved data when MSAA is on.
I am wondering if it is possible to always enable storage bit? Or is it due to some limitations of hardware / drivers (e.g. simply cannot have multisampling and storage functions on at the same time, etc.)?

Currently, there _may_ be a workaround by implementing a graphics pipeline in the compositor effect.

#### Explanation on using graphics pipeline in the compositor (w/ MSAA)

See this for example on implementing a full screen triangle-based graphics draw call, which translates stencil data into another texture (which is bound as the color attachment of the framebuffer): [Link](https://github.com/dmlary/godot-demo-sencil-buffer-compositor-effect).

(Tip: Remember that you can render into the same framebuffer with multiple pipelines and arbitrary numbers of geometry)

Note that, at the moment of writing, this example does not work with MSAA, though. But I figured out how to modify it to construct an MSAA-compatible graphics pipeline at least: basically, you need to use render_data.get_texture('render_buffers', 'color_msaa' / 'depth_msaa'), or turn the msaa argument to true when using get_xxx_layer, etc.

Then, you need to change the number of samples option when creating the texture to be used as color attachment, and also change the number of samples option when creating the attachment info structures (on each and every `RDAttachmentFormat`s - make them match those used by the depth buffer comming from the vanilla pipelines of godot). Also, don't forget to configure the RDPipelineMultisampleState accordingly when creating the frame buffer (failing to do so would likely result in things being rendered to only part of the screen).

This way, the framebuffer could be constructed without error, and you will be able to extract the stencil data into arbitrary render target (you may want to resolve it with `RenderingDevice.texture_resolve_multisample` into another buffer, make sure to follow the doc of that function on setting up the usage bits correctly, then).

#### Current workaround of Rendering back into the MSAA buffer?

Base on the above information, I guess it should be possible to create another graphics pipeline, where the unresolved msaa color buffer is bound as the color attachment, and then transfer any data you wish back into the msaa color buffer with fragment shaders running on a full-screen triangle. But I haven't actually tried this out, I'll post it here if there's any interesting follow-ups.

#### Background

I am working on some sort of home-made GI which is specialized to my use case (highly regular and small - medium scale grid-based maps). It maintains some 3D volumes in the compositor effect, and exposes them to regular graphics pipeline of godot through Texture3DRD. Everything worked well when I was sampling them in the fragment shaders. But When I tried to limit the computation to screen space (to ensure 0 overdraw, etc.), this problem occurs.

My compositor effect runs in past-opaque.

Godot version is 4.5 release.

PS. Is there any way other than using a graphics pipeline to access the stencil buffer? Currently, the compositor effect does not work very elegantly with either MSAA or stencil buffer...
